        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A dehumidifier held at a verified 50 percent will clear a musty basement in four to six weeks in most cases, because it stops the mold and bacteria producing the compounds you are smelling and then lets the absorbed reservoir outgas. Measure that 50 percent with a separate hygrometer in the corner that smells, not with the readout on the machine, which reads the driest air in the room.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Alongside the drying, remove what cannot be dried. Cardboard stacked against the foundation wall, carpet pad on a cold slab, drywall that was wet at the bottom, and damp fabric in storage are reservoirs that outlast any machine. If six verified weeks at 50 percent has not done it, stop adjusting the setpoint and go looking for a water source or a colonised material instead.
          </p>
        </div>

